Ladybird rejects at parse time a var() whose name isn’t a single
<custom-property-name> (var(--x 0px), var(name), var(--), var(var(--n)),
e.g.) and an env() whose name isn’t an identifier followed by
non-negative integers. Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 give
identical results on all 19 lines of the test, as Ladybird now does.

That departs from the current spec and from upstream WPT. Since a
WebKit change in August 2026 (bug 320896, “variable reference name
substitution”), the argument grammar of var() takes any
<declaration-value> as the name, and css/css-variables/var-parsing.html
and variable-reference.html upstream expect “var(--x ())” and
“var(--prop 20px)” to be valid at parse time. WebKit 26.5, the
shipping release, still rejects them, as do Chrome and Firefox.
Upstream css/css-env/env-parsing.html made the same change for env()
in November 2026. Ladybird’s imported copies of var-parsing.html and
env-parsing.html are the older versions; with this change, all of
their cases pass, and so do six more in indexed-env.tentative.html and
syntax.tentative.html.
